Quick one. Our dedupe helper reorders data and callers noticed. Save /workspace/dedupe.py:

def dedupe(items):
    return list(set(items))

Set iteration order is arbitrary across runs for strings (hash randomization), so output order is unstable and definitely not first-seen order. Write tests/test_dedupe.py first: dedupe(['b','a','b','c','a']) must equal ['b','a','c'] exactly, and run pytest a couple of times with different PYTHONHASHSEED values (0 and 1 via env in a subprocess, or just document the instability) to show the current implementation cannot guarantee it. Fix with dict.fromkeys to preserve first-seen order, add cases with ints mixed in a homogeneous test, an empty list, and a 10k-element performance sanity check that finishes instantly. Green.

